{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem explicitly asks 'what exactly distinguishes an idempotent operation from a safe one' drawing on published definitions. SRC_001 never states the distinction. It merely labels it 'real but moot' and pivots entirely to the deduplication-key mechanism. Acknowledging a distinction exists without articulating its content is a direct failure to answer the first half of the problem as stated, regardless of whether the second half (which property the log requires) is answered well.","target":"04aeef0fff33f5fe88d36a01f53971912b440630c9e8fb8b549f6aa272e51a48"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 frames safety as an 'intensional property (about the operation's internal execution not producing harmful side effects)' and idempotency as 'extensional.' This intensional/extensional split is not grounded in published definitions as the problem requires. The standard Alpern-Schneider definition of safety is a property that holds on every finite prefix of an execution trace — it is defined over observable execution histories, not over internal implementation details. Characterizing safety as 'intensional' about 'internal execution' misrepresents the published definition the problem asks to draw on.","target":"7839a7837628a446fd83255ec673e8472ee9484e890afe36fb8be2bf8db0626d"}]}